      "findings": [
        {
          "finding": "The change is safe because every tracked file that can weaken a check or add a dependency now has the code owner. CODEOWNERS owns nested lint and format configs, the security scanner config, Metro configs, and the CocoaPods and Bundler files, and the controls rule routes the same globs. tools/__tests__/codeowners.test.js reads the real CODEOWNERS and routing.json and fails when a tracked controls file or one of ten listed paths, tracked or new, lacks the owner. With the CODEOWNERS from before the fix it fails 11 of 14 cases. git ls-files with the owned patterns as an exclude file gives the same 118 owned files as the test's matcher.",
          "cites": "How sure are you 4",
          "commit": "efa7d33",
          "patch": "9b73e3f2492fb34ecc58efad55431c5d7a2b844d"
        },
        {
          "finding": "A change to a Podfile, Gemfile, Metro config, or nested lint, format, or scanner config now routes blast-radius and no longer qualifies for the tiny tier. The routing test now expects controls for a Podfile change. project.pbxproj, .xcode.env, the xcconfig and plist files, packages/server/.gitignore, and the Android Gradle files stay without a code owner.",
          "cites": "Steps 4",
          "none": "The routing change is intended and the routing test pins it. Native project files stay with normal review by design."
        },
        {
          "finding": "tools/github/integration-rules.json now equals the live ruleset, rule by rule, after adding the rebase-only merge method, the extra approval for unattributed changes, and the empty reviewer list. ruleset.cjs passes the parameters through unchanged, and ruleset.test.js still passes.",
          "cites": "Steps 3",
          "commit": "1d0b488",
          "patch": "939db07c8e93d864f4a36a724ae503acfaeb0564"
        }
      ],
